gosh seam 20: OCB's interface is the cipher's inside, in Crypto::Imp, and no longer in no namespace.

For:   REQ22/SHAPE-1, the core factored into domains by the seam. The cipher's ABI (crypto.h)
       includes OCB's interface (imp/ae.h) to declare one private field, Session's ae_ctx*; and
       that interface, Krovetz's, in C, declared everything in no namespace, so every user of the
       cipher (the transport, gosh's meetup) saw OCB's functions and struct as the program's own.
       seam/DOMAINS allowed it until now; the allow goes.
What:  Renames of where names are, and nothing else: no byte of the protocol, and no behaviour,
       changes. ae.h's declarations, still extern "C", are put in Crypto::Imp; the definitions in
       ocb_internal.cc and ocb_openssl.cc are wrapped in the same namespace. A function of C
       linkage has its name whatever namespace declares it, so the symbols compiled and linked
       (ae_init, ae_encrypt, ae_decrypt, ae_clear, ae_ctx_sizeof) are the same, and so are their
       instructions (ocb_internal.cc compiled before and after, -O2: only where the file-local
       helpers lie differs, their names being in Crypto::Imp). Session's field
       says Imp::ae_ctx; crypto.cc, the cipher's own, says using namespace Crypto::Imp.
       ocb_internal.cc's test program (OCB_TEST_PROGRAM, not built) says the same.
Where: src/crypto/crypto.h; src/crypto/imp/{ae.h,crypto.cc,ocb_internal.cc,ocb_openssl.cc}.
Kind:  no file moved; one interface's names in Crypto::Imp; one field and one user qualified.
From:  the core at the pin: src/crypto/ae.h, src/crypto/ocb_internal.cc, src/crypto/ocb_openssl.cc
       (moved under imp/ by patch 12).
Pin:   upstream mosh at decd9b705eb81626f694335b8d5940538beb06da; cut against the result of the patch before it.

